    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Collagen for men works exactly as it works for women, because collagen is the
        structural protein of tendon, ligament, cartilage, bone and skin, and that protein is not
        sex specific.</strong> The best studied protocol for people who train is 15g of collagen
        with vitamin C taken about an hour before loading, which roughly doubled a blood marker of
        collagen synthesis in a 2017 trial.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Best use case: connective tissue.</strong> Tendon, ligament and cartilage are where the trial evidence for men who lift, run or play is strongest. Doses used were 10g to 15g daily.</li>
        <li><strong>Timing matters more here than anywhere else.</strong> The tendon protocol is collagen plus vitamin C 30 to 60 minutes before loading, not after. The training session is what drives the peptides into the tissue.</li>
        <li><strong>Skin and hair benefits are not a female-only effect.</strong> The same peptides raise skin elasticity and hydration in men, and men lose collagen density with age from a higher starting point.</li>
        <li><strong>It will not build muscle.</strong> Collagen is a poor muscle-building protein, missing tryptophan and low in leucine. If you want hypertrophy, that is a whey or dal-and-paneer question, not a collagen one.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

