      <h2 id="gendered">Is collagen for men different from collagen for women?</h2>

      <p>
        No. Collagen is the protein that gives tendon, ligament, cartilage, bone and skin their
        tensile strength, and its structure is the same in both sexes. There is no men's collagen
        molecule and no women's collagen molecule, only different marketing and, occasionally,
        different co-factors bolted onto the same peptide base.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two real biological differences are worth naming, because they change what you should
        expect rather than what you should take. Men start with denser skin collagen. Dermal
        thickness is higher in men across most of adult life, and the decline is gradual rather
        than stepped. Women lose a large slice of skin collagen in the first years after menopause,
        which is why the decade-by-decade picture differs, and why we cover it separately in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-women">collagen for women</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The second difference is load. Men in my clinic present with connective tissue that has
        been asked to carry more absolute weight, often with less flexibility to start with, and
        usually with a history of stopping and restarting training rather than training
        continuously. That pattern, not sex hormones, is what determines whether a tendon holds up
        at 42.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the honest framing is this. Collagen for men is not a different product. It is the same
        peptide with a different priority order: joints and tendons first, bone underneath that,
        skin and hair as a genuine but secondary benefit that most men are quietly pleased about
        and rarely mention at the counter.
      </p>

      <h2 id="opd">What men actually walk into my OPD with</h2>

      <p>
        Three presentations account for most of the men who ask me about collagen, and none of them
        is a cosmetic complaint. Gym knees and loaded shoulders in the twenties and thirties,
        overuse from cricket and recreational running, and desk-job stiffness that shows up in the
        forties as a knee that objects to stairs.
      </p>

      <p>
        The gym group is usually squatting or pressing with a volume their tendons have not caught
        up to. Muscle adapts fast. Tendon adapts slowly, because tendon has poor blood supply and a
        collagen turnover measured in months. A man who adds 20kg to his squat over eight weeks has
        given his quadriceps eight weeks of adaptation and his patellar tendon considerably less.
        That mismatch is the most common cause of anterior knee pain I see in otherwise healthy
        young men.
      </p>

      <p>
        The cricket and running group presents with Achilles pain, shoulder pain from throwing, or
        a shin that hurts on the first kilometre and settles by the third. These are loading
        problems with a nutritional component, not nutritional problems with a loading component.
        Get that order right before you buy anything.
      </p>

      <p>
        The forties group is the largest. Ten hours seated, a joint that has not moved through full
        range in years, and cartilage that is fed only by movement. I have written the full
        cartilage explanation in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for joint
        pain</a>, so I will not repeat it here. The short version: rest is often the wrong
        prescription, and a supplement without a strength programme is a wasted purchase.
      </p>

