      <h2 id="tendons">Does collagen for men help tendons, ligaments and recovery?</h2>

      <p>
        This is the strongest part of the case, and it is the part most men have never heard.
        Collagen taken with vitamin C shortly before mechanical loading appears to increase the
        rate at which the body builds new collagen in tendon and ligament. The mechanism is not
        mysterious: you supply the amino acids, then you apply the stimulus that tells the tissue
        where to put them.
      </p>

      <p>
        The reference experiment is Shaw and colleagues, published in the American Journal of
        Clinical Nutrition in 2017. Participants took 5g or 15g of vitamin C enriched gelatin an
        hour before six minutes of rope skipping. Blood taken afterwards was applied to engineered
        ligament in the laboratory. The 15g group showed roughly double the collagen synthesis
        marker of the control group, and the engineered tissue was mechanically stronger.
      </p>

      <p>
        I want to be careful about what that study is and is not. It is a small mechanistic study
        using gelatin rather than hydrolysed peptides, and its main outcome is a blood marker plus
        a laboratory tissue model, not a torn tendon that healed faster in a human being. It has
        been influential because the mechanism is plausible and the protocol is cheap to try, not
        because it settled the question.
      </p>

      <p>
        Clinical work has followed. Praet and colleagues reported in 2019 that collagen peptides
        added to an eccentric loading programme improved outcomes in Achilles tendinopathy faster
        than the loading programme alone, over six months. Again the supplement sat on top of the
        exercise. Nobody has shown collagen doing anything useful for a tendon that is not being
        loaded.
      </p>

      <p>
        For cartilage rather than tendon, the numbers come from Clark's 24 week trial in 147
        athletes at 10g daily, and from Zdzieblik's 2017 work in young adults with knee pain at 5g
        daily for 12 weeks. Both found reduced activity-related pain. Both took at least three
        months.
      </p>

