      <p>
        One India-specific note. A vegetarian man eating a typical north Indian diet is often
        sitting at 0.6g to 0.8g of protein per kg of body weight, well under the 1.2g to 1.6g that
        supports training. If that is you, collagen is not your first purchase. Sort out total
        protein from dal, rajma, paneer, curd and soya, then add collagen for the tissue that whey
        does not serve.
      </p>

      <h2 id="skin-hair">Does collagen do anything for men's skin and hair?</h2>

      <p>
        Yes for skin, with the same modest and reasonably well replicated effect seen in women. A
        2014 double blind trial by Proksch and colleagues found measurable improvement in skin
        elasticity after eight weeks of daily collagen peptides, and later meta-analyses have
        pointed the same way for hydration and elasticity.
      </p>

      <p>
        Indian men have a particular version of this problem. Outdoor commuting, high UV exposure
        through most of the year, and almost no sunscreen use in the male population add up to
        photoaging that arrives earlier than it should. That is ultraviolet damage to the dermal
        collagen network, and while a supplement supports the building side of the equation, it
        cannot outpace the damage side. Sunscreen is the higher yield intervention, and it is
        cheaper. The detail sits in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">collagen for skin</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hair is where I would push back hardest on the marketing. Collagen is not a component of
        the hair shaft, which is built from keratin. It is present in the connective tissue sheath
        around the follicle, and there is a reasonable argument about the dermal environment, but
        no trial has shown oral collagen reversing male pattern baldness. Androgenetic alopecia is
        driven by dihydrotestosterone and genetics, and it has actual treatments that work. If
        that is your concern, read <a href="/pages/collagen-for-hair">collagen for hair</a> and
        then see a dermatologist rather than buying a shot.
      </p>

      <h2 id="best">Best collagen for men: how to read the label</h2>

      <p>
        Judge a product on dose and co-factors, never on the packaging colour or the word
        "men" on the front. Four things decide whether a collagen product is worth buying, and
        three of them are printed on the back of the box.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>First, the dose in milligrams.</strong> The trials used 5g to 15g. A capsule
        product delivering 500mg per serving is delivering a twentieth of a studied dose, whatever
        the front of the pack says. This is the single most common way collagen products fail, and
        it is why the tablets versus shots question is really a dose question, covered in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-tablets-vs-shots">collagen tablets vs capsules vs shots</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Second, is it hydrolysed?</strong> Hydrolysis breaks collagen into short peptides
        that survive digestion and reach the bloodstream. Unhydrolysed collagen in a gram-level dose
        is largely wasted. The exception is undenatured type II collagen at around 40mg, which
        works through a completely separate immune pathway, explained in
        <a href="/pages/types-of-collagen">types of collagen</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Third, is vitamin C in the formula?</strong> Vitamin C is a required co-factor for
        the enzymes that cross-link collagen. Without it, the body cannot build stable collagen from
        the amino acids you have supplied. Any collagen product without vitamin C either expects you
        to supply it or has not thought about the chemistry.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Fourth, what else is in there, and at what dose?</strong> A joint formula should be
        honest about whether its co-factors are at therapeutic doses or supporting ones. Skelecoll
        contains 300mg of glucosamine HCl, for instance, where the standalone glucosamine trials
        used 1,500mg. That is a supporting co-factor inside a stack built on collagen, not a
        glucosamine therapy, and I would rather say so than let you assume otherwise. The full
        ingredient-by-ingredient grading is in
        <a href="/pages/supplements-for-knee-pain">supplements for knee pain</a>.
      </p>

