      <p>
        A practical schedule for a man who trains four days a week: shot an hour before the
        session on training days, same time each morning on rest days so the habit does not break.
        Consistency beats timing precision on the days you are not loading anything. Take it with
        water or juice, chilled, and do not treat it as a substitute for a meal.
      </p>

      <p>
        Expect nothing in week one. Morning stiffness and the first few steps out of a chair
        usually ease before pain intensity changes. Function improves through weeks four to eight.
        The published pain reductions landed at eight to twelve weeks. If twelve weeks of daily use
        alongside sensible training has changed nothing, stop and spend the money elsewhere. That is
        a legitimate outcome and it happens.
      </p>

      <h2 id="limits">Who should not bother</h2>

      <p>
        Some men should not buy this, and some should see a doctor instead. I would rather lose a
        sale than have a man treat a mechanical problem with a supplement for six months.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Anyone with a hot, red, swollen joint.</strong> That pattern suggests infection or acute inflammatory arthritis and needs assessment now, not nutrition.</li>
        <li><strong>Anyone whose knee locks or gives way.</strong> That is usually a meniscal or ligament problem. No supplement addresses a torn structure.</li>
        <li><strong>Anyone with a fish or shellfish allergy.</strong> Skelecoll uses marine collagen. This one is not negotiable.</li>
        <li><strong>Men whose total protein intake is inadequate.</strong> Collagen at 10g will not fix an overall deficit. Food first.</li>
        <li><strong>Men expecting muscle gain, fat loss or hair regrowth.</strong> The evidence does not support any of those, and I am not going to pretend otherwise.</li>
        <li><strong>Men on prescription medication or with kidney disease.</strong> Ask your treating doctor before adding any protein supplement.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        There is also a category I would call low priority rather than contraindicated: a healthy
        25 year old with no joint symptoms, no heavy training load and a good diet. Collagen will
        not hurt him. It is simply not the best use of 2,199 rupees a month at that stage of life.
        Spend it on food, sleep and a gym membership he actually uses.
      </p>

