      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Is collagen good for men?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes, particularly for joints, tendons and bone. Collagen is the structural protein of
            connective tissue and it behaves identically in men and women. Trials in athletes used
            10g daily for 24 weeks and reported less activity-related joint pain.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is the best collagen for men?</summary>
          <p>
            The best collagen for men is whichever product gives you 5g to 15g of hydrolysed
            collagen per serving with vitamin C alongside it. Ignore the word "men" on the pack and
            read the milligram figure on the back. Skelecoll provides 10,000mg per 30ml shot with
            40mg of vitamin C from amla.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen build muscle in men?</summary>
          <p>
            No. Collagen contains no tryptophan and very little leucine, the amino acid that
            triggers muscle protein synthesis, so it is a poor muscle-building protein. Use whey,
            eggs, paneer or dal for muscle and collagen for the connective tissue around it.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should men take collagen before or after a workout?</summary>
          <p>
            Before, if tendons or ligaments are your reason for taking it. Shaw and colleagues used
            15g with vitamin C about an hour before loading and saw roughly double the collagen
            synthesis marker. For general joint support the timing matters far less than taking it
            every day.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen help men's hair loss?</summary>
          <p>
            No trial has shown oral collagen reversing male pattern hair loss. Hair shafts are made
            of keratin, not collagen, and androgenetic alopecia is driven by DHT and genetics.
            Collagen may support the skin around the follicle, which is a much smaller claim than
            the marketing usually makes.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can men take collagen and whey protein together?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes, and for most men who train that is the sensible combination. Whey supplies leucine
            for muscle, collagen supplies glycine and proline for tendon, ligament and cartilage.
            Take collagen before training and whey after if you want to follow the study protocols
            closely.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

